IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `ip-nouns`

-- Antitrust Guidelines for the Licensing of Intellectual Property (2017), part 4 (but
-- 4.3, which is ip-safety-zone.l4): the rule of reason.
--
-- AGENCY ENFORCEMENT POLICY, NOT LAW. Part 4 is mostly considerations the agencies weigh
-- qualitatively ("necessarily a qualitative one", 4.2). Where the text lists
-- considerations, the caller supplies them and the module reports which are present; it
-- never weighs them. Where the text states a definite step ("they will not challenge
-- the restraint"), the module takes that step on the agencies' findings.

§ `Part 4: the rule of reason`

§§ `4.1.1 Market structure, coordination and foreclosure`

DECLARE `Consideration bearing on the risk of harm (4.1.1, 5.4)` IS ONE OF
    `concentration of the relevant markets`
    `difficulty of entry`
    `low responsiveness of supply and demand to changes in price`
    `a large proportion of the relevant markets affected by the restraint`
    `the long duration of the restraint`
    `similar restraints imposed by owners of competing technologies`
    `common restraints on competing licensees`

-- 4.1.1 [pdf pages 21-22]: the potential for harm "depends in part on the degree of
-- concentration in, the difficulty of entry into, and the responsiveness of supply and
-- demand to changes in price in the relevant markets"; the risk of foreclosure "is
-- related to the proportion of the markets affected ...; other characteristics of the
-- relevant markets ...; and the duration of the restraint"; similar or common restraints
-- may make coordination easier. 5.4 [pdf page 31] names the same for exclusive dealing.
-- A report of those present, in the text's order: not a weighing, and not a finding of
-- harm ("The use of similar restraints may be common and procompetitive in an industry").
@ref 4.1.1 [pdf pages 21-22]; 5.4 [pdf page 31]
GIVEN m IS A `Market characteristics (4.1.1, 5.4)`
GIVETH A LIST OF `Consideration bearing on the risk of harm (4.1.1, 5.4)`
`4.1.1: the considerations bearing on the risk of harm that are present` MEANS
    catMaybes (LIST (IF m's `the relevant markets are concentrated` THEN JUST `concentration of the relevant markets` ELSE NOTHING),
                    (IF m's `entry into the relevant markets is difficult` THEN JUST `difficulty of entry` ELSE NOTHING),
                    (IF m's `supply and demand respond little to changes in price` THEN JUST `low responsiveness of supply and demand to changes in price` ELSE NOTHING),
                    (IF m's `the restraint affects a large proportion of the relevant markets` THEN JUST `a large proportion of the relevant markets affected by the restraint` ELSE NOTHING),
                    (IF m's `the restraint is of long duration` THEN JUST `the long duration of the restraint` ELSE NOTHING),
                    (IF m's `owners of competing technologies impose similar restraints on their licensees` THEN JUST `similar restraints imposed by owners of competing technologies` ELSE NOTHING),
                    (IF m's `competing licensees are subject to common restraints from a common licensor or competing licensors` THEN JUST `common restraints on competing licensees` ELSE NOTHING))

§§ `4.1.2 Licensing arrangements involving exclusivity`

-- 4.1.2 [pdf pages 22-24]: the first form of exclusivity, in effect as well as in form.
-- "A non-exclusive license may have the effect of exclusive licensing if it is
-- structured so that the licensor is unlikely to license others or to practice the
-- technology itself." Choosing a single licensee, confining activity to one field or
-- place, and having only one licensee are not read: they do not by themselves raise the
-- concern (4.1.2 [pdf page 24]).
@ref 4.1.2 [pdf pages 22-24]
GIVEN l IS A `License terms and structure (4.1.2)`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `4.1.2: the license has the effect of exclusive licensing` IF
       l's `the license by its terms is exclusive or partially exclusive, limiting the licensor's ability to license others`
    OR l's `the license is structured so that the licensor is unlikely to license others or to practice the technology itself`

-- 4.1.2 [pdf pages 23-24], 5.4 [pdf page 31]: the second form, exclusive dealing, "may be
-- achieved by an explicit exclusive dealing term in the license or by other provisions
-- such as compensation terms or other economic incentives". That licensees choose the
-- licensed technology to the exclusion of others does not by itself foreclose
-- competition (4.1.1 [pdf page 22]) and is not read.
@ref 4.1.2 [pdf pages 23-24]; 5.4 [pdf page 31]
GIVEN l IS A `License terms and structure (4.1.2)`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `4.1.2: the license has the effect of exclusive dealing` IF
       l's `a term of the license prevents or restrains the licensee from licensing, selling, distributing or using competing technologies`
    OR l's `the license is structured to increase significantly the licensee's cost when it uses competing technologies`

DECLARE `What section 4.1.2 says of the license` IS ONE OF
    `generally, the exclusive licensing raises no antitrust concern: there is no horizontal relationship among licensors, among licensees, or between the licensor and a licensee`
    `generally, the non-exclusive license does not present antitrust concerns: it contains no restraint on the competitive conduct of the licensor or the licensee`

-- 4.1.2 [pdf page 23]: "Generally, such exclusive licenses may raise antitrust concerns
-- only if there is a horizontal relationship among licensors, or among licensees, or
-- between the licensor and its licensee(s)", and "A non-exclusive license of
-- intellectual property that does not contain any restraints on the competitive conduct
-- of the licensor or the licensee generally does not present antitrust concerns. That
-- principle holds true even if the parties to the license are in a horizontal
-- relationship". The "only if" states nothing when a horizontal relationship exists;
-- nor does the text state an outcome for a non-exclusive license with a restraint:
-- NOTHING in both cases. Exclusive dealing is a restraint on the licensee's conduct, and
-- is assessed under 5.4; for an exclusive license this result is about the exclusive
-- licensing only.
@ref 4.1.2 [pdf page 23]
GIVEN l IS A `License terms and structure (4.1.2)`
      h IS A `Horizontal relationships among the parties (4.1.2)`
GIVETH A MAYBE `What section 4.1.2 says of the license`
`4.1.2: what the agencies say of the license's exclusivity` MEANS
    BRANCH
      IF exclusive AND (NOT horizontal) THEN JUST `generally, the exclusive licensing raises no antitrust concern: there is no horizontal relationship among licensors, among licensees, or between the licensor and a licensee`
      IF exclusive THEN NOTHING
      IF (NOT `4.1.2: the license has the effect of exclusive dealing` l) AND (NOT l's `the license contains another restraint on the competitive conduct of the licensor or the licensee`)
        THEN JUST `generally, the non-exclusive license does not present antitrust concerns: it contains no restraint on the competitive conduct of the licensor or the licensee`
      OTHERWISE NOTHING
  WHERE
    exclusive MEANS `4.1.2: the license has the effect of exclusive licensing` l
    horizontal MEANS
           h's `there is a horizontal relationship among licensors`
        OR h's `there is a horizontal relationship among licensees`
        OR h's `there is a horizontal relationship between the licensor and a licensee`

§§ `4.2 Efficiencies and justifications`

DECLARE `What section 4.2 says the agencies do` IS ONE OF
    `the agencies will not challenge the restraint`
    `the agencies will not give weight to the parties' efficiency claim`
    `the agencies will balance the procompetitive efficiencies and the anticompetitive effects to determine the probable net effect on competition in each relevant market`

-- 4.2 [pdf pages 25-26]: "If the Agencies conclude ... that a restraint ... is unlikely to
-- have an anticompetitive effect, they will not challenge the restraint. If ... it has,
-- or is likely to have, an anticompetitive effect, they will consider whether the
-- restraint is reasonably necessary ... If the restraint is reasonably necessary, the
-- Agencies will balance ..." and "If it is clear that the parties could have achieved
-- similar efficiencies by means that are significantly less restrictive, then the
-- Agencies will not give weight to the parties' efficiency claim."
-- The balance is qualitative and the agencies' own: the module stops at "will balance".
-- The text states no outcome for a restraint likely to be anticompetitive that is not
-- reasonably necessary and has no clear alternative (NOTHING). A finding that the
-- restraint is reasonably necessary although a significantly less restrictive
-- alternative is clear contradicts itself (NOTHING). Duration is a factor in reasonable
-- necessity, reported below, not a step.
@ref 4.2 [pdf pages 25-26]
GIVEN f IS A `Findings under the rule of reason`
GIVETH A MAYBE `What section 4.2 says the agencies do`
`4.2: what the agencies do on their findings` MEANS
    BRANCH
      IF f's `the agencies conclude that the restraint is unlikely to have an anticompetitive effect`
        THEN JUST `the agencies will not challenge the restraint`
      IF alternative AND necessary THEN NOTHING
      IF alternative THEN JUST `the agencies will not give weight to the parties' efficiency claim`
      IF necessary THEN JUST `the agencies will balance the procompetitive efficiencies and the anticompetitive effects to determine the probable net effect on competition in each relevant market`
      OTHERWISE NOTHING
  WHERE
    alternative MEANS f's `it is clear that the parties could have achieved similar efficiencies by practical and significantly less restrictive means`
    necessary MEANS f's `the restraint is reasonably necessary to achieve procompetitive efficiencies`

DECLARE `Consideration of reasonable necessity (4.2)` IS ONE OF
    `a practical and significantly less restrictive alternative`
    `a duration clearly exceeding the period needed to achieve the efficiency`

-- 4.2 [pdf pages 25-26]: the two considerations the text names for whether a restraint is
-- reasonably necessary, reported when present. The agencies "will not attempt to draw
-- fine distinctions regarding duration".
@ref 4.2 [pdf pages 25-26]
GIVEN f IS A `Findings under the rule of reason`
GIVETH A LIST OF `Consideration of reasonable necessity (4.2)`
`4.2: the considerations against reasonable necessity that are present` MEANS
    catMaybes (LIST (IF f's `it is clear that the parties could have achieved similar efficiencies by practical and significantly less restrictive means` THEN JUST `a practical and significantly less restrictive alternative` ELSE NOTHING),
                    (IF f's `the duration of the restraint clearly exceeds the period needed to achieve the procompetitive efficiency` THEN JUST `a duration clearly exceeding the period needed to achieve the efficiency` ELSE NOTHING))
